From 30ee967aacce74c50b4080bb370450a78367fd75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:55:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] check every conjunct after the guard of a
 quantification

The ACSL parser associates conjunctions to the left, so
`\exists integer n; 0 <= n < size && P && Q` is
`((0 <= n && n < size) && P) && Q`. When visit_quantif_relations found the
end of the guard inside the left operand of a conjunction, it returned P as
the goal, and the caller discarded it and took the right operand Q as the
goal: every conjunct but the last one was dropped, even when parenthesized
(the parser re-associates `G && (P && Q)` too). A false predicate such as
`\exists integer n; 0 <= n < 5 && n == 1000 && n >= 0` then passed.

The goal is now the conjunction of the goal found in the left operand and
the right operand.

In universal quantifications, visit_quantif_relations also accepted an
implication inside the hypothesis of an outer implication, as in
`\forall integer n; (0 <= n < size ==> P) ==> Q` or
`\forall integer i, j; (0 <= i < n ==> 0 <= j < m) ==> i < n`, and a
conjunction outside an implication hypothesis, as in
`\forall integer i, j; 0 <= i < n ==> 0 <= j && (j < m ==> i < n)`,
turning false predicates into valid ones. An `in_lhs` flag now tracks
whether the traversal is inside the hypothesis of an implication: nested
implications inside a hypothesis and conjunctions outside a hypothesis are
rejected, while right-nested implications such as
`\forall integer i, j; 0 <= i < n ==> 0 <= j < m ==> P` remain accepted.

Regression test: tests/constructs/quantif_conjunctions.c.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
